<script>
    // 1. Data Structure (Simulating Extraction Phase)
    const courseContent = [
        {
            type: 'section',
            title: 'Quick Definition',
            icon: '📖',
            content: `
                <p>The <strong>Law of Conservation of Mechanical Energy</strong> states that the total mechanical energy in a closed system (isolated from external non-conservative forces like friction) remains constant.</p>
                <br>
                <p>This means energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another—specifically, between <strong>Kinetic Energy</strong> (motion) and <strong>Potential Energy</strong> (position).</p>
            `
        },
        {
            type: 'formula',
            id: 'formula-main',
            latex: 'E_{mech} = KE + PE = \\text{constant}',
            title: 'The Conservation Equation',
            variables: [
                { symbol: 'E_{mech}', meaning: 'Total Mechanical Energy' },
                { symbol: 'KE', meaning: 'Kinetic Energy' },
                { symbol: 'PE', meaning: 'Potential Energy' }
            ],
            explanation: `
                <p>This equation is the heart of the concept. It tells us that if you add the energy of motion ($KE$) and the energy of position ($PE$) at any point in time, the sum will always be the same number.</p>
                <p>Mathematically, this implies:</p>
                $$ \\Delta KE + \\Delta PE = 0 $$
                <p>Any gain in kinetic energy must be accompanied by an equal loss in potential energy, and vice versa.</p>
            `
        },
        {
            type: 'section',
            title: 'Key Concepts',
            icon: '🔑',
            content: `
                <ul style="padding-left: 1.2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;">
                    <li><strong>Kinetic Energy (KE):</strong> The energy an object possesses due to its motion. Depends on mass and velocity ($KE = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$).</li>
                    <li><strong>Potential Energy (PE):</strong> Stored energy based on position. In gravity fields, it depends on mass, height, and gravity ($PE = mgh$).</li>
                    <li><strong>Conservative Forces:</strong> Forces (like gravity) where the work done does not depend on the path taken.</li>
                    <li><strong>Isolated System:</strong> For the law to hold perfectly, there should be no air resistance or friction.</li>
                </ul>
            `
        },
        {
            type: 'formula',
            id: 'formula-expansion',
            latex: '\\frac{1}{2}mv_1^2 + mgh_1 = \\frac{1}{2}mv_2^2 + mgh_2',
            title: 'Detailed Energy Balance',
            variables: [],
            explanation: `
                <p>This is the practical form used to solve problems. It equates the total energy at state 1 (point 1) to the total energy at state 2 (point 2).</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 1.2rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                    <li><strong>v</strong>: Velocity</li>
                    <li><strong>h</strong>: Height relative to a reference point</li>
                    <li><strong>m</strong>: Mass (which cancels out if it doesn't change)</li>
                </ul>
            `
        },
        {
            type: 'section',
            title: 'Example: Free-Falling Ball',
            icon: '🎾',
            content: `
                <p>Imagine a 2kg ball dropped from a height of 10 meters. Let's look at the energy at the top, halfway down, and just before impact (ignoring air resistance).</p>
                <br>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Position</th>
                                <th>Height (h)</th>
                                <th>Velocity (v)</th>
                                <th>PE (Joules)</th>
                                <th>KE (Joules)</th>
                                <th>Total (Joules)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Top</td>
                                <td>10 m</td>
                                <td>0 m/s</td>
                                <td>196 J</td>
                                <td>0 J</td>
                                <td><strong>196 J</strong></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Middle</td>
                                <td>5 m</td>
                                <td>9.9 m/s</td>
                                <td>98 J</td>
                                <td>98 J</td>
                                <td><strong>196 J</strong></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Bottom</td>
                                <td>0 m</td>
                                <td>14 m/s</td>
                                <td>0 J</td>
                                <td>196 J</td>
                                <td><strong>196 J</strong></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            `
        },
        {
            type: 'section',
            title: 'Important Notes',
            icon: '⚠️',
            content: `
                <p>In the real world, mechanical energy is rarely perfectly conserved because of <strong>non-conservative forces</strong> like friction and air resistance. These forces convert mechanical energy into thermal energy (heat) and sound.</p>
                <br>
                <p>However, assuming conservation is an excellent approximation for many physics problems involving roller coasters, pendulums, and orbits.</p>
            `
        }
    ];

    // 2. Rendering System
    const contentArea = document.getElementById('content-area');

    function renderContent() {
        courseContent.forEach(block => {
            if (block.type === 'section') {
                createSectionCard(block);
            } else if (block.type === 'formula') {
                createFormulaCard(block);
            }
        });

        // Render Math after DOM is ready
        renderMathInElement(document.body, {
            delimiters: [
                {left: '$$', right: '$$', display: true},
                {left: '$', right: '$', display: false}
            ],
            throwOnError: false
        });
    }

    function createSectionCard(block) {
        const card = document.createElement('article');
        card.className = 'card';
        card.innerHTML = `
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">${block.icon}</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">${block.title}</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-content">
                ${block.content}
            </div>
        `;
        contentArea.appendChild(card);
    }

    function createFormulaCard(block) {
        const card = document.createElement('article');
        card.className = 'card formula-card';
        card.innerHTML = `
            <div class="card-header" style="border-bottom-color: #fde68a;">
                <div class="card-icon" style="background: #fff; color: #d97706;">📐</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">${block.title}</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="formula-display">
                $$${block.latex}$$
            </div>
            <div class="formula-actions">
                <button class="btn btn-outline" onclick="toggleExplanation('${block.id}')">
                    Explain Formula
                </button>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="openFormulaLab()">
                    ⚡ Open Formula Lab
                </button>
            </div>
            <div id="explain-${block.id}" class="explanation-box">
                ${block.explanation}
            </div>
        `;
        contentArea.appendChild(card);
    }

    // 3. Interaction Logic
    window.toggleExplanation = function(id) {
        const el = document.getElementById(`explain-${id}`);
        el.classList.toggle('active');
    };

    // 4. Formula Lab (Simulation Logic)
    const labDialog = document.getElementById('formula-lab');
    const closeLabBtn = document.getElementById('close-lab');

    // Simulation State
    const sim = {
        m: 2.0,
        L: 1.5,
        g: 9.8,
        angle: Math.PI / 4, // Initial angle (45 degrees)
        velocity: 0,
        dragging: false,
        originX: 0,
        originY: 0,
        scale: 100 // pixels per meter
    };

    // Canvas Setup
    const canvas = document.getElementById('simCanvas');
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    let animationFrame;

    function resizeCanvas() {
        const rect = canvas.parentElement.getBoundingClientRect();
        canvas.width = rect.width;
        canvas.height = rect.height;
        sim.originX = canvas.width / 2;
        sim.originY = 50;
    }

    window.openFormulaLab = function() {
        labDialog.showModal();
        resizeCanvas();
        if (!animationFrame) animate();
    };

    closeLabBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        labDialog.close();
    });

    // Lab Controls
    const inpMass = document.getElementById('inp-mass');
    const inpLen = document.getElementById('inp-length');
    const inpGrav = document.getElementById('inp-gravity');

    function updateParams() {
        sim.m = parseFloat(inpMass.value);
        sim.L = parseFloat(inpLen.value);
        sim.g = parseFloat(inpGrav.value);

        document.getElementById('lbl-mass').textContent = sim.m.toFixed(1) + ' kg';
        document.getElementById('lbl-length').textContent = sim.L.toFixed(1) + ' m';
        document.getElementById('lbl-gravity').textContent = sim.g.toFixed(1) + ' m/s²';
    }

    [inpMass, inpLen, inpGrav].forEach(el => el.addEventListener('input', updateParams));

    // Physics Engine
    function updatePhysics(dt) {
        if (sim.dragging) {
            sim.velocity = 0;
            return;
        }

        // Simple Pendulum Equation: angular acceleration = -(g/L) * sin(theta)
        const acceleration = -(sim.g / sim.L) * Math.sin(sim.angle);

        // Semi-implicit Euler integration
        sim.velocity += acceleration * dt;
        sim.angle += sim.velocity * dt;

        // Damping (air resistance approximation for stability)
        sim.velocity *= 0.999; 
    }

    // Drawing Engine
    function draw() {
        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

        // Calculate bob position
        const bobX = sim.originX + Math.sin(sim.angle) * sim.L * sim.scale;
        const bobY = sim.originY + Math.cos(sim.angle) * sim.L * sim.scale;

        // Draw Pivot
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(sim.originX, sim.originY, 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fillStyle = '#64748b';
        ctx.fill();

        // Draw String
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(sim.originX, sim.originY);
        ctx.lineTo(bobX, bobY);
        ctx.strokeStyle = '#334155';
        ctx.lineWidth = 2;
        ctx.stroke();

        // Draw Bob
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(bobX, bobY, 15 + sim.m * 2, 0, Math.PI * 2); // Size varies slightly with mass
        ctx.fillStyle = '#2563eb';
        ctx.fill();
        ctx.strokeStyle = '#1e40af';
        ctx.lineWidth = 2;
        ctx.stroke();

        // Update UI Stats
        updateStats();
    }

    function updateStats() {
        // Height relative to lowest point (h = L - Lcos(theta))
        const h = sim.L * (1 - Math.cos(sim.angle));

        // PE = mgh
        const pe = sim.m * sim.g * h;

        // KE = 0.5 * m * (v_linear)^2
        // v_linear = L * angular_velocity
        const v_linear = sim.L * sim.velocity;
        const ke = 0.5 * sim.m * v_linear * v_linear;

        const total = pe + ke;

        // Update DOM
        document.getElementById('val-pe').textContent = pe.toFixed(1) + ' J';
        document.getElementById('val-ke').textContent = ke.toFixed(1) + ' J';
        document.getElementById('val-total').textContent = total.toFixed(1) + ' J';

        // Update Bars
        const maxEnergy = total > 0.1 ? total : 1; // Prevent division by zero
        document.getElementById('bar-pe').style.width = (pe / maxEnergy * 100) + '%';
        document.getElementById('bar-ke').style.width = (ke / maxEnergy * 100) + '%';
    }

    let lastTime = 0;
    function animate(timestamp) {
        if (!lastTime) lastTime = timestamp;
        const dt = Math.min((timestamp - lastTime) / 1000, 0.05); // Cap dt for stability
        lastTime = timestamp;

        updatePhysics(dt);
        draw();

        if (labDialog.open) {
            animationFrame = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
        } else {
            animationFrame = null;
        }
    }

    // Mouse Interaction for Lab
    canvas.addEventListener('mousedown', (e) => {
        const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
        const mouseX = e.clientX - rect.left;
        const mouseY = e.clientY - rect.top;

        const bobX = sim.originX + Math.sin(sim.angle) * sim.L * sim.scale;
        const bobY = sim.originY + Math.cos(sim.angle) * sim.L * sim.scale;

        const dist = Math.hypot(mouseX - bobX, mouseY - bobY);
        if (dist < 30) {
            sim.dragging = true;
            sim.velocity = 0;
        }
    });

    window.addEventListener('mousemove', (e) => {
        if (sim.dragging && labDialog.open) {
            const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
            const mouseX = e.clientX - rect.left;
            const mouseY = e.clientY - rect.top;

            const dx = mouseX - sim.originX;
            const dy = mouseY - sim.originY;
            sim.angle = Math.atan2(dx, dy);
        }
    });

    window.addEventListener('mouseup', () => {
        sim.dragging = false;
    });

    // Initialize
    renderContent();

</script>
</body>
